WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:06.080
Welcome back to the news you missed in 2020,
from every country in the world. If you haven’t

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.380
yet seen part one, make sure you go and watch
that first, unless, of course, you only care

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.639
about the small countries, because we’ll
start with one with a rather petite population

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.100
of just eight and a half million.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:22.110
An 8-year-old boy who tried to use “spirit
money”—fake bills used in China as spiritual

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.020
offerings—at a grocery store in Switzerland
didn’t get Monopoly’s “Go to Jail”

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.970
card, but he did get a mugshot and now has
a police record. The heavy-handed punishment

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:36.020
for using counterfeit money surprised residents
because of the boy’s age and presumed naivety,

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.250
and caused some embarrassment for the country,
which discussed the case openly in parliament.&nbsp;

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:47.460
Chevron’s $4 billion acquisition of Texas-based
Noble Energy was an American transaction,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.290
but was rooted in Israel. That’s because
Noble Energy has built a large natural gas

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.739
business in and around Israeli waters, and
the purchase essentially opens up the country

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:01.110
to energy development—a nation that had
remained virtually untouched to such activities

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:06.010
in order to maintain political stability with
Arab oil-producing countries. Not only does

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:10.460
the deal unlock the potential for a brand
new resource economy, but it demonstrates

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.810
that the Persian Gulf may now be more accepting
of Israeli business.&nbsp;

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.360
Togo became the first country in Africa to
eliminate “sleeping sickness,” a sometimes

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.409
fatal parasitic infection transmitted by flies,
found primarily in rural areas in 36 African

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:31.450
countries. Thanks to more than two decades
of dedicated public health measures, the West

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:36.700
African country eliminated the illness, which
causes fever, aches, and, most commonly, disrupts

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.229
a person’s sleep. The good news is that
cases are also dropping throughout Africa,

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:45.900
and the number of people dying has dropped
90% since the early ‘90 when the annual

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.420
death-toll reached a peak of 34,000.&nbsp;

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.340
Some more good news out of Africa is that
girls and young women who are pregnant in

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.810
Sierra Leone are no longer banned from attending
school. Previously, once pregnant, young women

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:03.479
were not allowed to finish school or take
their exams, but this decision is a victory

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.890
for feminism in the country, and will allow
tens of thousands of young women to potentially

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.660
complete their education and provide them
with more opportunity in life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:17.950
The crater of a meteorite that hit earth 800,000
years ago, blasting glass particles known

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:24.110
as tektites to over 10% of the planet, had
gone unfound... until now. Researchers think

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:29.530
they have located the impact crater—stretching
11 miles or 17 kilometers long by 8 miles

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.810
or 13 kilometers wide and filled with about
300 feet or 90 meters of rock—on a plateau

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.319
in Laos. Though a crater like this should
have presented itself through erosion over

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:45.130
eons, this elusive hole was covered by more
than 1,000 feet or 300 meters of lava flow,

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:50.270
and was only discovered on a hillside after
it was carved out to pave a road.&nbsp;

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:55.280
Brazilian World Cup football star Ronaldinho
spent the past six months in Paraguayan jail

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.671
and house arrest for entering the country
in March on an illegal passport. The star

00:02:59.671 --> 00:03:04.459
player spent more than a month behind bars,
and was then transported to a luxury hotel

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:08.430
and held there for another four months, while
his legal troubles were sorted out between

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:13.150
the governments—he says he didn’t know
the passport was fake. During the latter part,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:18.129
he reportedly had parties and sang karaoke,
leading people to wonder if prison is really

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:19.800
that bad.&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.739
Speaking of fake passports and punishment,
Raya, a three-month-old chihuahua, was facing

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:29.540
euthanasia for traveling from her home country
of Bulgaria to Norway, where she’d been

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:35.400
adopted, on a falsified passport until action
star Jean-Claude Van Damme stepped in, and

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.200
you can’t make this stuff up. Bulgaria initially
refused to take the dog back, until Van Damme

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.890
took to social media, and the country capitulated,
eventually repatriating her and putting her

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.330
up for adoption again.&nbsp;

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:51.041
Remember that story about Israel, natural
gas, and the Mediterranean Sea? We’re back

00:03:51.041 --> 00:03:55.739
to it, but this time, with Lebanon involved.
The two countries started mediation for the

00:03:55.739 --> 00:04:01.340
first time in 30 years over part of the sea
because of their dual interest in energy development.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:07.280
Both say a 330 square mile, 850 square kilometer
area of the sea is part of their exclusive

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.510
economic zone. The two have been essentially
at a standstill for decades, and even though

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:16.220
they had to communicate through mediators
from the United States, their meeting marked

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.419
an historic moment.&nbsp;

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:22.789
More mediation in the Middle East actually
took place at the United Nations in Geneva,

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:27.520
but it led to a ceasefire between the two
main warring factions in Africa’s Libya.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.969
The two sides have been engaged in a bloody
battle for years, and this newest peace attempt

00:04:31.969 --> 00:04:35.979
brings some hope to both the country, and
to nearby nations that have previously tried

00:04:35.979 --> 00:04:39.080
to intervene, such as Russia and Turkey.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.750
As Nicaragua continues to develop its natural
resources, reports show that it may be doing

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:48.749
it at the cost of indigenous people’s lives.
The government is actively promoting illegal

00:04:48.749 --> 00:04:52.860
land grabs for timber and mining companies,
and there have been reports that more than

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.250
40 native people have been killed—though
the police refuse to investigate. It’s all

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:01.749
the more complicated by the fact that President
Daniel Ortega has family ties to one of the

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:05.669
most active timber companies working in the
protected area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:10.960
Up the coast, Guatemala offered El Salvador
the opportunity to build a port on its lands

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.339
and waters, giving Atlantic Ocean access to
El Salvador, whose coastline is only on the

00:05:16.339 --> 00:05:21.349
Pacific. In its initial phases, the port will
likely be constructed through a private-public

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:26.159
partnership and infrastructure, such as hotels
and warehouses, would be built around the

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:31.389
port as well, bringing a significant El Salvadorian
presence to its neighboring country.&nbsp;

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:36.840
Alright, try to keep up with this one: In
Kyrgyzstan, Central Asia’s only democracy,

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.580
the president resigned, after a week in hiding,
following a tumultuous election. A few hours

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.189
before his announcement, President Jeenbekov
said he would not step down amid the disputed

00:05:47.189 --> 00:05:51.860
election, but then he did, and it left some
wondering if his quick about-face was triggered

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:57.330
by threats of violence from a criminal-backed
group. The new leader is a former kidnapper

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:02.189
who was sprung from jail by anti-government
protestors at the same time as Jeenbekov’s

00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:04.529
resignation, raising many eyebrows.&nbsp;

00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:10.169
Nearby, the president of Turkmenistan is widely
known for his obsession with a breed of dog

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:16.181
called the Alabai. So it didn’t come as
a surprise that he erected a 19-foot, 6-meter

00:06:16.181 --> 00:06:21.279
gold statue of the shepherd-like canine in
a traffic circle in the country’s capital.

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:25.919
Turkmenistan is also known to have one of
the world’s more repressive governments,

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:30.569
and doesn’t even allow human rights organizations
into the country. That creates a definite

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:35.949
disconnect in terms of humanizing animals,
but treating humans inhumanely.&nbsp;

00:06:35.949 --> 00:06:41.099
Investors and entrepreneurs have keyed in
on a new use for Australia’s desolate and

00:06:41.099 --> 00:06:45.929
unforgiving outback: turn it into a solar
farm large enough to power the country of

00:06:45.929 --> 00:06:52.759
Singapore. Sun Cable has announced a $20 billion,
10-gigawatt, 30,000-acre solar farm that will

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:58.680
send about ⅔ of the energy it produces north
through undersea high-voltage cables. If all

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:04.059
goes right, the lights of Singapore could
be solar-powered by 2027.

00:07:04.059 --> 00:07:08.610
In another part of the global movement to
cleaner energy, Denmark, the European Union’s

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:13.249
largest oil producer, announced it would end
all oil and gas exploration in the North Sea

00:07:13.249 --> 00:07:19.370
as part of its commitment to phase out fossil
fuel extraction by 2050. Its existing 55 platforms,

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:24.469
spread across 20 oil and gas fields, will
continue operations, but the decision to stop

00:07:24.469 --> 00:07:29.050
looking for more sends a powerful message
to the world about the future of energy and

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.319
this country’s goals to be a green leader.&nbsp;

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:36.909
Some of the world may be saying goodbye to
fossil fuels, but an animal that oddly turns

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:42.979
out to like gas-powered vehicles, or at least
their sounds, is a monkey. A zoo in Finland

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:47.709
gave their white-faced saki monkeys a choice
between the sounds of traffic, the jungle,

00:07:47.709 --> 00:07:53.029
zen, or dance music, and the primates overwhelmingly
preferred honking cars and screeching tires

00:07:53.029 --> 00:07:56.520
over the mimicry of their natural habitat.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:01.379
When Slovakian president Zuzana Caputova learned
that Chinese telecom giant Huawei was the

00:08:01.379 --> 00:08:05.649
sponsor for a defense conference at which
she was slated to speak, she quickly pressed

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:10.779
end call and canceled her talk. She cited
security risks, along with following her basic

00:08:10.779 --> 00:08:15.369
principles, since Huawei has been accused
of foreign interference. Huawei eventually

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:19.689
backed out of the conference and Caputova
ended up taking the stage, but the message

00:08:19.689 --> 00:08:22.429
was heard loud and clear.

00:08:22.429 --> 00:08:27.059
The World Wildlife Foundation has been working
for more than a decade to set up a national

00:08:27.059 --> 00:08:32.039
park and nature reserve in the Republic of
the Congo, however, this year, the European

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:36.640
Union pulled their funding upon reports that
the anti-poaching guards patrolling the proposed

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.009
park have been engaging in physical violence
against the indigenous people of the area,

00:08:41.009 --> 00:08:46.519
and are restricting them from moving around
on their own land. The World Wildlife Foundation’s

00:08:46.519 --> 00:08:50.810
application for EU funding specifically stated
that the indigenous people of the area were

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:55.970
for the project, but given that that either
no longer, or was never true, it’s unclear

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:59.190
whether this project will move forward.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:04.829
Only three countries still allow commercial
whaling, and Norway is one of them. Typically

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:09.650
the Scandanavian country sends most of its
whale meat to Japan, where demand is high,

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:13.790
but Norwegians are opting to consume more
of the meat for the first time in years, which

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:18.520
is probably due to the growing movement against
traditionally processed meat in favor of locally

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.399
sourced products, like the minke whales they
hunt in their own waters.&nbsp;

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:27.910
Close your eyes if you don’t want to see
something bizarre: It’s the wrinkle-faced

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:34.731
bat, native to Costa Rica. But of course what
one sees as ugly, others find… sexy? New

00:09:34.731 --> 00:09:39.720
research this year found that these elusive
jungle-dwellers mate in an interesting way:

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.500
The males congregate in big groups, perform
a routine, and fold some of the extra skin

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:51.029
around their face up like a neck gaiter to
seduce the females… and, it seems to work.&nbsp;

00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:55.400
Three Palestinian peace activitsts who held
a Zoom call with Israelis that included more

00:09:55.400 --> 00:10:01.040
than 200 participants and viewers were charged
with “weakening revolutionary spirit.”

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.600
Their goal was part of a bridge-building initiative
called Skype with Your Enemy, but according

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.920
to Palestinian officials, communication with
Israel is considered a “normalization”

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:17.360
activity and punishable. The three could face
years in prison and hard labor sentences.&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.620
The Arab world’s longest-serving ruler,
the Sultan of Oman, died this year at age

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:28.259
of 79. Since he had no children, his cousin
was chosen to replace him, ending years of

00:10:28.259 --> 00:10:34.279
speculation on which of the more than 80 potential
successors would gain the spot. Upon his passing,

00:10:34.279 --> 00:10:39.829
the Sultan was recognized for his steadfast,
diplomatic reign by other world leaders. This

00:10:39.829 --> 00:10:44.740
is a seemingly peaceful story about a leadership
change, amid a flurry of headlines this year

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:46.640
that were quite the opposite.&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.261
It’s easier to get to Liberia from France,
as well as other Western destinations, now

00:10:51.261 --> 00:10:56.410
that Air France resumed its flight from Paris
to Monrovia after a nine-year hiatus. The

00:10:56.410 --> 00:11:01.800
direct flight debuted on an Airbus a350, which
landed at a newly refurbished Robert International

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.139
Airport in Liberia. Officials boasted about
the newly improved connections between the

00:11:06.139 --> 00:11:11.019
United States, United Kingdom, India, and
China with the return of this flight, and,

00:11:11.019 --> 00:11:15.140
naturally, the strengthened ties between Liberia
and France.&nbsp;

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:19.930
When visitors to the North Pole buried a time
capsule there in 2018, they thought it would

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:25.000
be 40 years until it was discovered, but,
because of climate change and the fast-melting

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:30.000
Arctic, the “artifacts” were found much
sooner; they washed up on the shores of Ireland

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.129
this year, some 2,300 miles or 3,700 kilometers
away. That’s obviously another bad sign

00:11:36.129 --> 00:11:40.139
for the Arctic, which some scientists say
will be ice-free by the summer of 2035.&nbsp;

00:11:40.139 --> 00:11:47.790
Last year, a volcano on New Zealand’s White
Island erupted and killed 22 people, including

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:53.259
children and seniors, who were there with
tour groups. Now, 13 parties have been charged

00:11:53.259 --> 00:11:57.860
in their deaths, with lawsuits claiming negligence
for allowing people to visit the site of an

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:03.040
active volcano. A family owns the island,
and tours were run by a small group of operators,

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.380
but the island has been closed to visitors
ever since.&nbsp;

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:11.641
A Sudanese militia leader who had been on
the run for 13 years was arrested in a remote

00:12:11.641 --> 00:12:16.339
corner of the Central African Republic, near
the border with Sudan, which is where he allegedly

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:21.089
carried out crimes against humanity, including
mass campaigns of muder, rape, and looting

00:12:21.089 --> 00:12:26.390
in the early 2000s, which partially contributed
to a humanitarian crisis in which 300,000

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:31.810
people were killed and 2.7 million were driven
from their homes. His arrest and subsequent

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:37.420
trial at the Hague will potentially bring
justice to many who have suffered in Darfur.&nbsp;

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:42.240
For several months, Mauritania banned Senegal
from fishing in its waters because of illegal,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:48.329
unreported, and unregulated fishing, which
they say cost their country up to $300 million

00:12:48.329 --> 00:12:53.300
in lost revenue. Now, under the new agreement,
Senegal will pay Mauritania for the actual

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:58.389
fish it catches, which means no more punitive
fines for the country instead. It seems like

00:12:58.389 --> 00:13:02.949
a win-win for the two West African countries
and demonstrates some progress towards countries

00:13:02.949 --> 00:13:06.550
all happily sharing the same pond.&nbsp;

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:11.690
In 2016, the Panama Papers exposed how some
of the world’s most prominent politicians,

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:15.550
business leaders, and celebrities set up offshore
bank accounts to conceal their wealth and

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:21.139
avoid taxes in the Central American country.
So, of course, Netflix made a movie about

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:26.560
it. But now, the law firm behind the 11.5
million documents is not happy about their

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.970
portrayal as “ruthless, uncaring, and unethical
lawyers,” so they’re doing the most lawyer

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:38.249
thing possible and suing Netflix. Jürgen
Mossack and Ramón Fonseca are claiming defamation

00:13:38.249 --> 00:13:41.970
and asked for Netflix to cease streaming “The
Laundromat,” though it’s already been

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.529
screened to crowds at European film festivals.&nbsp;

00:13:44.529 --> 00:13:49.850
Readers, writers, and free speech advocates
are rejoicing now that officials in Kuwait

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:55.269
disbanded a committee that banned more than
5,000 books over seven years. The country’s

00:13:55.269 --> 00:13:59.829
Ministry of Information will no longer have
the control to censor titles like Victor Hugo’s

00:13:59.829 --> 00:14:04.610
“The Hunchback of Notre Dame” and “One
Hundred Years of Solitude” by Gabriel García

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:09.939
Márquez. But, besides banning book imports,
the committee also had a say over every title

00:14:09.939 --> 00:14:14.509
that was published in the country. Now they
don’t, and that means the words should flow

00:14:14.509 --> 00:14:15.509
more freely.&nbsp;

00:14:15.509 --> 00:14:19.680
Here’s a tip: If you’re German, but wanted
in the United States for building software

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.950
that tricks automobile emissions inspectors,
then don’t cross from Germany, where you’re

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:28.779
protected, into Croatia, where you’re not.
Apparently, former Volkswagen executive Alex

00:14:28.779 --> 00:14:33.220
Eiser didn’t get the memo that only his
native country doesn’t extradite its own

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:37.040
citizens. He was arrested when crossing the
border in June, and will likely be charged

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.809
in the United States since America and Croatia
signed an extradition treaty last year. He’s

00:14:41.809 --> 00:14:45.600
at the center of the emissions scandal in
which the German auto manufacturer tried to

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.879
evade US Clean Air laws by tricking the system,
and his arrest shows that the United States

00:14:50.879 --> 00:14:54.389
isn’t finished with this car chase yet.&nbsp;

00:14:54.389 --> 00:14:59.470
Moldova is veering away from its Putin-leaning
ways, at least temporarily, with its election

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:05.760
of Maia Sandu, a pro-Western, Harvard-educated
former prime minister, and its ouster of incumbent,

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.260
pro-Russian president Igor Dodon. Besides
favoring European ties, Sandu is also the

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:15.470
country’s first woman president. She ran
on an anti-corruption platform and championed

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:20.269
embracing the country’s Romanian roots—as
75% of the country speaks Romanian—over

00:15:20.269 --> 00:15:25.220
its ties to Russia. Tiny Moldova is sandwiched
between Ukraine and Romania, which have also

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:29.560
leaned more Western in recent years, and the
results demonstrate this region’s increased

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:31.709
shift away from Russia.&nbsp;

00:15:31.709 --> 00:15:35.970
If you’re tired of hearing about Georgia,
the US state, you can opt to move to the Eastern

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:41.699
European country of the same name to escape
the 24/7 news. The nation recently announced

00:15:41.699 --> 00:15:45.930
a visa-free entry program, called Remotely
From Georgia, that grants year-long stays

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:51.430
to nationals of 95 countries. There are some
caveats: transplants must earn at least $2,000

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:55.300
per month and have health insurance, meaning
the country is looking for a certain type

00:15:55.300 --> 00:16:00.839
of more permanent visitor—one that has money
and can contribute to its faltering economy.&nbsp;

00:16:00.839 --> 00:16:04.810
The European Union is being held accountable
for the way it funds projects in Africa, thanks

00:16:04.810 --> 00:16:09.879
to a group of Eritreans in the Netherlands.
They allege that the EU is paying for forced

00:16:09.879 --> 00:16:13.990
labor by funding heavy construction equipment
to open and pave a road that connects the

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:19.089
Ethiopia-Eritrea border with the Eritrean
port of Massawa. That equipment is apparently

00:16:19.089 --> 00:16:24.649
being run by conscripts. The project is part
of a $6 billion injection from the EU into

00:16:24.649 --> 00:16:30.059
Africa as a way to curb migration from south
to north, but this case shows that more oversight

00:16:30.059 --> 00:16:32.410
is needed in the process.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.649
Another immigration story centers around a
steady stream of Argentinians relocating to

00:16:36.649 --> 00:16:41.829
Uruguay, a move that the latter is quite happy
about. Uruguay enticed residents from its

00:16:41.829 --> 00:16:45.959
southern neighbor by lowering the minimum
value of property required for tax residency

00:16:45.959 --> 00:16:53.279
from $1.7 million to $380,000, and by offering
a 10-year “tax holiday.” That, in turn,

00:16:53.279 --> 00:16:58.319
has led close to 20,000 people to flock north
this year, a number equivalent to about 0.6%

00:16:58.319 --> 00:17:05.679
of Uruguay’s population of 3.5 million,
meaning the great pandemic migration continues.&nbsp;

00:17:05.679 --> 00:17:10.329
Perhaps signaling that Bosnia-Herzegovina
is next on the luxury destination list, Saudi

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:15.679
developer Dar Al Arkan announced this year
it would build a 5-million-square-foot residential

00:17:15.679 --> 00:17:20.699
and hotel complex outside of Sarajevo, making
it the largest real estate project in the

00:17:20.699 --> 00:17:24.649
country’s history. This addition is being
touted as a second-home community that will

00:17:24.649 --> 00:17:30.500
attract both Bosnian and international visitors,
and expand the developer’s global footprint.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:35.140
When much of the world was brought to a standstill,
a Mongolian cuckoo made its most epic journey

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:41.179
yet: an 8,000 mile or 13,000 kilometer return
trip from South Africa to Mongolia, completing

00:17:41.179 --> 00:17:46.351
a roundtrip journey that’s one of the longest
ever recorded for a land bird. Scientists

00:17:46.351 --> 00:17:50.899
tracked the bird using a GPS tag to study
its habits, and were frankly surprised it

00:17:50.899 --> 00:17:56.429
survived the year away, and 27 border crossings.
Though long-distance birds aren’t revelatory,

00:17:56.429 --> 00:18:00.500
the lengths to which this species went are
notable, and perhaps poses more questions

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:04.480
than answers, such as, why go so far?&nbsp;

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:09.850
The Armenia-Azerbaijan war populated headlines
this fall, but notably absent from those stories

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:14.649
were the United States’ involvement in the
conflict. Prior to the Trump administration,

00:18:14.649 --> 00:18:19.149
the United States actively engaged in previous
peace negotiations in the region, but the

00:18:19.149 --> 00:18:23.400
America First doctrine that dominates the
current administration led to diplomatic disengagement

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.860
there. That said, the two countries still
agreed to a ceasefire, which has already been

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:32.650
tested and broken, according to Russian peacekeepers,
and now we’ll see if the Biden administration

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:35.480
plans to become more involved.&nbsp;

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:41.179
Move over rum, Jamaica is looking to a new
economic driver: bamboo. The government announced

00:18:41.179 --> 00:18:46.150
efforts to boost bamboo production on the
island nation, which it sees as a more sustainable

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:51.440
revenue source. Bamboo is a quick-growing
plant that can be used as an alternative to

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:56.490
traditional wood products in tissues, paper,
and cloth. Caribbean island nations are, in

00:18:56.490 --> 00:19:01.230
some cases, pushing for greener futures out
of necessity—in 2019 Jamaica banned plastic

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:05.120
bags and styrofoam—and also opportunity.&nbsp;

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:08.970
The EU’s getting a lot of attention, and
this time it’s because North Macedonia and

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:14.400
Albania have restarted negotiations to join
the 27-member institution. The two Balkan

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:19.000
countries were put on hold for two years after
some EU members questioned their track records

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.220
regarding democracy and corruption, but those
concerns have been assuaged and there’s

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:27.980
renewed momentum for the two to join neighbors
Serbia, Kosovo, Montenegro, and Bosnia in

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:33.179
entering the EU. Even with Great Britain’s
exit, the EU motto these days seems to be:

00:19:33.179 --> 00:19:34.850
the more the merrier.&nbsp;

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:39.809
Qatar is Australia’s third biggest destination
for lamb exports, behind the US and China,

00:19:39.809 --> 00:19:44.330
but that’s likely to change with the Arab
country canceling its subsidy of meat from

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:49.500
Down Under. The subsidies, which support this
$300-million-a-year trade, were set to expire

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:55.470
in 2023, but Qatar abruptly announced their
end this year. Some are linking the decision

00:19:55.470 --> 00:20:00.650
to Australia’s criticism of Qatar’s treatment
of 13 Australian women earlier this year,

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:05.529
who were questionably strip-searched at Doha
International Airport. Qatar says that’s

00:20:05.529 --> 00:20:09.690
not it, and rather it’s to bolster their
sustainability and independence.&nbsp;

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:14.690
If you’re one of those people who are sketched
out by large bodies of water and their unknown

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:19.900
contents, this won’t provide comfort. In
Lithuania, a team of divers completing a survey

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:24.210
for bridge repairs came across the remains
of a 16th century soldier, including leather

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:28.809
boots with spurs, a leather belt with a buckle,
and an iron sword. Though the current bridge

00:20:28.809 --> 00:20:34.529
was built in 1934, records indicate there
was also one in its place 500 years ago. It’s

00:20:34.529 --> 00:20:39.250
the first underwater remains to be found in
Lithuania, and researchers were impressed

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:42.830
by how well-preserved the skeleton was.&nbsp;

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:49.029
Know anyone looking for a pet elephant? Well,
Namibia has 170 of them for sale. That’s

00:20:49.029 --> 00:20:53.170
because, despite the mammal being on the endangered
species list, they’re seeing a population

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:57.420
rebound in the south African country thanks
to successful conservation measures, jumping

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:05.620
from 7,500 in 1995 to 24,000 in 2019. But,
with their growth comes an increase in human-elephant

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:11.150
conflict as well as a fight for natural resources
during a drought year. Auctioning off some

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:16.799
of the pachyderms may alleviate some of the
stress and raise funds for the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.799 --> 00:21:22.010
President Adama Barrow assumed leadership
in The Gambia in 2017, following 22 years

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:26.450
of a brutal dictatorship, and at the time
he promised to call for new elections after

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:32.700
three years, instead of serving the full five-year
term. Well, guess what? Now he wants the full

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:36.990
five. Earlier this year, protestors took to
the streets to show their disappointment,

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:43.169
and in turn, 137 of them were arrested and
dozens more were detained. For some residents,

00:21:43.169 --> 00:21:46.590
the show of force seemed like a harkening
back to the country’s darker days, and it

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:49.899
looks like Barrow will be staying in power.&nbsp;

00:21:49.899 --> 00:21:55.110
The second-largest diamond ever mined was
unearthed in 2019, but it caused headlines

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:59.790
this year for its subsequent sale. It wasn’t
a diamond house or British royal that bought

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:05.380
the 1,758-carat gem, but French company Louis
Vuitton that purchased it from the Karowe

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:10.220
mine in Botswana. The sale isn’t only notable
because of its price—no numbers have been

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:15.179
released but some estimate around $50 million—but
because the buyer is a relative newcomer to

00:22:15.179 --> 00:22:20.760
the super high-end jewelry market and this
boss move signals they’re here to stay.&nbsp;

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:25.299
Scientists in Gabon discovered their own treasure
this year, finding the largest pangolin ever

00:22:25.299 --> 00:22:30.630
on record and tagging it for research purposes.
The scaly mammal is thought to be one of the

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:34.770
most frequently illegally trafficked animals
in the world—with much of the demand coming

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:38.680
from Asia—and researchers are hoping that
by learning about the creature’s habits

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:42.630
and movements, they’ll be better able to
protect it as demand increases for both its

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:47.169
meat and bones, which are often used in Chinese
medicine.&nbsp;

00:22:47.169 --> 00:22:52.010
Former Lesotho prime minister Thomas Thabane
has denied any role in his ex-wife’s murder,

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:57.130
despite police reports that say that he previously
paid a $20,000 down payment for her killing,

00:22:57.130 --> 00:23:03.890
which was part of a $180,000 total sum. His
ex-wife was shot in a car, two days before

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:08.309
his inauguration. Before that, she rejected
getting a divorce so that she could remain

00:23:08.309 --> 00:23:13.529
the First Lady. He remarried two months after
her death, but was forced to resign from power

00:23:13.529 --> 00:23:15.399
amid the controversy.&nbsp;

00:23:15.399 --> 00:23:20.350
North Macedonia, which only became “North”
in 2019 to differ itself from the Greek region

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:25.880
of Macedonia, has the potential to delineate
itself through marijuana production too—if

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:30.320
the government will support it. Though medical
marijuana has been legal in the country since

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:35.600
2016, there’s a new push to legalize cannabis
in all forms, and to ramp up production to

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.320
supply Western European countries with pot,
where sale and consumption are permitted in

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:44.750
modest quantities—but huge grow operations
are not. Toward the end of 2020, government

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:50.779
officials signaled their support to make North
Macedonia a “cannabis superpower.”

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:55.950
Burning and removing statues has been a thing
this year, and a wood depiction of Melania

00:23:55.950 --> 00:24:01.309
Trump erected in her hometown of Sevnica,
Slovenia was no exception. Someone set fire

00:24:01.309 --> 00:24:06.059
to the statue in July, so the artist took
the reasonable action of replacing it in more

00:24:06.059 --> 00:24:11.250
permanent bronze. That was probably a good
idea, since the small town of 5,000 residents

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:17.480
has seen upwards of 20,000 Melania-tourists
a year since 2016, when President Trump took

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.390
office.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:23.779
Vultures may not be the cutest birds, but
they’re one the animal kingdom needs for

00:24:23.779 --> 00:24:28.720
their scavenger role. Unfortunately, the hooded
vulture in Guinea-Bissau inched closer to

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:33.919
extinction this year, with the accidental
poisoning of more than 1,000 of the birds.

00:24:33.919 --> 00:24:38.640
There, residents put strychnine in the landfills
to ward off feral dogs, with the unintended

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:43.910
consequence of it being consumed by the scavenging
birds. This actually happens a lot in Africa,

00:24:43.910 --> 00:24:48.470
and the vulture population across the continent
is highly threatened because of it.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:53.900
A woman in Latvia made headlines for taking
10 days off after her child was born—because

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:58.929
she took it as paternity leave. Previously,
same-sex couples in the country had not been

00:24:58.929 --> 00:25:03.580
granted the right to both take parental leave,
but she fought the ruling as unconstitutional

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:08.010
and was the first woman in the country to
use her paternity leave.&nbsp;

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:11.769
Arab countries trying to get the attention
of Europeans are doing it in perhaps the most

00:25:11.769 --> 00:25:16.950
obvious way ever, through soccer—or football
for the rest of the world. That’s why Bahrain

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:21.540
purchased a minority stake in football club
Paris F.C. this year, with a direct goal to

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:25.559
attract more Europeans to the Gulf State,
which is still feeling the negative effects

00:25:25.559 --> 00:25:29.679
of stifling pro-democracy uprisings during
the Arab Spring and is therefore trying to

00:25:29.679 --> 00:25:33.049
patch up its global reputation.&nbsp;

00:25:33.049 --> 00:25:37.159
We hear a lot about the migration crises of
people traveling from Africa to Europe and

00:25:37.159 --> 00:25:40.909
Central America to the United States, but
a movement that gets less coverage is the

00:25:40.909 --> 00:25:47.269
one of Venezeulans --many of which go to Trinidad
and Tobago. By late 2020, there were almost

00:25:47.269 --> 00:25:52.570
20,000 registered refugees in the island nation,
but amid the ones who make it, there are tragic

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:58.450
stories too. Most recently, 11 Venezuelans,
including children, died in a boat trying

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:02.740
to cross the Gulf of Paria, which separates
Trinidad from the Venezuelan coast by just

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:05.149
under 7 miles or 11 kilometers.&nbsp;
&nbsp;

00:26:05.149 --> 00:26:09.250
When a constitutional referendum passed in
Guinea earlier this year that allowed the

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:13.269
president to run for a third term instead
of ending at two, it was certainly created

00:26:13.269 --> 00:26:17.620
with President Alpha Conde in mind. There
were protests then, and again when he was

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:22.950
re-elected in October with nearly 60% of the
vote. Since the results, dozens have died

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:27.730
in the demonstrations but that didn’t stop
him from being sworn into office earlier this

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:29.279
month.&nbsp;

00:26:29.279 --> 00:26:33.700
Cyber-warfare seems like something that happens
in the ether, but American operatives actually

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:38.250
traveled to Estonia prior to the American
election in November to learn about the northeastern

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:42.970
European country’s sophisticated defense
tactics. Specifically, the US had Russian

00:26:42.970 --> 00:26:47.620
hackers in mind, and Estonia has one of the
stronger defense networks because Russia used

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:51.029
to test its malware and disinformation campaigns
there.&nbsp;

00:26:51.029 --> 00:26:57.460
A whistleblower from a 2004 case between Australia
and East Timor continues to draw attention,

00:26:57.460 --> 00:27:00.850
and this year the lawyer for the anonymous
whistleblower, referred to as Witness K, won

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:06.799
a free speech prize for his work on the case.
Witness K and lawyer Bernard Colleary revealed

00:27:06.799 --> 00:27:11.020
that Australia was spying on conversations
between East-Timorese officials about oil

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:16.190
and gas negotiations in the Timor Sea. East
Timor is one of the poorer countries in the

00:27:16.190 --> 00:27:21.299
world, and the whistleblowing did help the
country get a fairer financial deal.&nbsp;

00:27:21.299 --> 00:27:26.679
Mauritius is an Indian Ocean island country
that sees 1.3 million visitors each year,

00:27:26.679 --> 00:27:31.809
many coming for its lagoons, tropical jungles,
and mountains, but a Japanese oil tanker ran

00:27:31.809 --> 00:27:37.659
ashore in July spilling 1,000 tons of fuel
into the sea, and since then more than 40

00:27:37.659 --> 00:27:42.019
dolphins have washed up on shore. Some are
worried the disaster could extend beyond dying

00:27:42.019 --> 00:27:46.799
dolphins and severely impact the country’s
environment and therefore its primary tourist

00:27:46.799 --> 00:27:49.370
draw--the pristine nature.&nbsp;

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:54.250
Those who are in, are in, but now that Cyprus
has shut the door on its golden passport program,

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:59.929
it will be harder for people to buy their
way into the European Union. More than 3,000

00:27:59.929 --> 00:28:04.289
foreigners who agreed to invest $2 million
each into the country benefited from the program

00:28:04.289 --> 00:28:09.470
in its seven-year existence, but new allegations
of--and no one could have guessed this--corruption

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:13.690
have led officials to suspend the program.
But that’s not to say it wasn’t worth

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:16.880
it--Cyprus raised $7 billion from it, all
told.&nbsp;

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:23.039
Eswatini joined an exclusive club this past
fall--countries that have consulates to Morocco

00:28:23.039 --> 00:28:28.389
in the Western Sahara. As covered earlier,
this is a disputed zone claimed both by Morocco

00:28:28.389 --> 00:28:33.350
and the Polisario Front, and many countries
choose not to state a strong position on the

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:37.909
issue. With the opening of a consulate to
Morocco in the Western Saharan city of Laayoune,

00:28:37.909 --> 00:28:42.539
however, the Kingdom of Eswatini is firmly
positioning itself on Morocco’s side, and

00:28:42.539 --> 00:28:46.910
it becomes the 15th country to open a consulate
to Morocco in the area.

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:51.010
Do you remember the chameleon that hadn’t
been seen for 100 years in Madagascar? There’s

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:54.880
another good story like it out of Djibouti,
where a mouse-sized elephant shrew has been

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:59.540
seen again for the first time in 50 years.
The good news in this case, unlike with the

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:04.120
chameleon, is that the shrew’s habitat isn’t
under threat so scientists finding 12 animals

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:08.880
from the 1,000 traps set hopefully means it
won’t be another 50 years until they’re

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:09.880
seen again.&nbsp;

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:14.530
When it comes to sharing good news, Fiji beat
out all other countries to become the first

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:18.899
nation to congratulate President-elect Joe
Biden on his victory. Fijian Prime Minister

00:29:18.899 --> 00:29:24.080
Frank Bainimarama sent out the celebratory
tweet before any other heads of state, but

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:29.230
it wasn’t purely altruistic. In the tweet,
he called for action on the planet’s climate

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:33.970
emergency, and asked Biden to bring the United
States back into the Paris Agreement.&nbsp;

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:38.070
Think back to the origins of the pandemic,
when the world still thought it would be confined

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:43.299
to China. That’s when the tiny island-nation
of the Comoros got some media attention for

00:29:43.299 --> 00:29:49.110
giving China 100 Euros as a show of solidarity
in fighting the virus. Of course, few places

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:53.860
would be untouched, and it did hit the Comoros
in late April, but ultimately the Chinese-Comoros

00:29:53.860 --> 00:29:57.970
relationship is ongoing, as the world’s
most populated country continues to invest

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:02.200
in the East African island as part of its
military and economic strategy.&nbsp;

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:07.419
Guyana is poised to be one of the world’s
next big oil producers, but there are some

00:30:07.419 --> 00:30:12.679
who aren’t happy about that potential. ExxonMobil
found sites that could potentially deliver

00:30:12.679 --> 00:30:17.850
8 billion barrels of oil, which could amount
to more than $20 billion in revenue for one

00:30:17.850 --> 00:30:23.000
of South America’s poorer countries. The
World Bank has pledged $55 million to help

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:27.500
the country in its development, but critics
say this opposes global goals of curbing fossil

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:28.650
fuel dependency.&nbsp;

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:34.701
China, India, and Bhutan are in ongoing negotiations
over their definitive borders—the 25th round

00:30:34.701 --> 00:30:38.490
of talks was delayed this year because of,
well, you know, everything—but that didn’t

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:43.690
stop impatient Chinese from constructing and
inhabiting a village 1 mile or 1.6 kilometers

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:48.669
within Bhutan’s recognized border this year.
This territorial grab is something China continues

00:30:48.669 --> 00:30:53.260
to do, especially to its immediate neighbors
such as Bhutan, which has just under 800,000

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:57.860
residents and poses little threat to the world
and military superpower.&nbsp;

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:02.679
What do China, Iran, North Korea, and the
Solomon Islands all have in common? Well,

00:31:02.679 --> 00:31:08.460
as of now, it’s a Facebook ban. The South
Pacific nation plans to join the three countries

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:12.899
in banning the social media site because,
according to officials, citizens are using

00:31:12.899 --> 00:31:18.580
it to abuse political leaders and access dangerous
information. Facebook was used as an organizing

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:22.480
tool for protests that crippled the nation’s
capital last year, and critics of the ban

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:26.990
say this is a move to stop criticism of the
government.&nbsp;

00:31:26.990 --> 00:31:31.889
The United States military is the strongest
in the world, and other countries want a piece

00:31:31.889 --> 00:31:40.140
of it. For $36 million, Montenegro was able
to buy a taste: 67 Joint Light Tactical Vehicles.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:44.580
The agreement is part of a NATO ally deal
that allows such transactions between countries,

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.760
and Montenegro is on the United States’
friendly list as Western allies try to massage

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:52.410
the region out of its Russian-dominated past.&nbsp;

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:57.420
Looking to ease traffic congestion, Luxembourg
became the first country in the world to offer

00:31:57.420 --> 00:32:03.289
public transportation entirely for free. Officials
say it could save households roughly $125

00:32:03.289 --> 00:32:08.159
per year, and also encourage public transportation
use versus private vehicles, which account

00:32:08.159 --> 00:32:14.120
for 71% of leisure travel and 47% of business.
A new tramway in the country’s capital is

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:19.029
one attractive alternative for riders. There
is an exception, though: first-class passengers

00:32:19.029 --> 00:32:20.950
will still have to pay.&nbsp;

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:25.500
South America’s Suriname is switching gears,
with the election of a new president who replaces

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:30.220
a dictator-turned-populist running on a poor
economic record and penchant for corruption.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:35.049
The new president, Chan Santokhi, assumes
leadership of a country on the brink of financial

00:32:35.049 --> 00:32:39.940
collapse and diplomatic disarray—in recent
years, the country turned to China and Venezuela

00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:44.289
for global ties, but Santokhi will be tasked
in realigning the country with its former

00:32:44.289 --> 00:32:46.129
imperial power, the Netherlands.&nbsp;

00:32:46.129 --> 00:32:51.649
When a Japanese couple on a world tour/honeymoon
were stranded due to pandemic travel bans

00:32:51.649 --> 00:32:56.299
on the 10 tiny islands off the coast of West
Africa known as Cape Verde, they settled in

00:32:56.299 --> 00:33:01.400
for five months and got to know the locals.
Now, they’ll be ambassadors for the nation

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:06.419
at the upcoming summer Olympics in Tokyo.
Cape Verde is sending three to four athletes,

00:33:06.419 --> 00:33:09.889
and though they’ve never won a medal in
the summer games, they will at least be well

00:33:09.889 --> 00:33:12.549
represented this time.&nbsp;

00:33:12.549 --> 00:33:17.360
Global superpowers continue to fight for strategic
power on islands throughout the world, and

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:21.570
this time, the United States has its eyes
on the Maldives, where it announced it is

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:27.809
opening an embassy. The string of 1,100 islands
is also in the crosshairs of India and China,

00:33:27.809 --> 00:33:31.600
which have financially supported the nation.
Particularly, China has lent the country millions

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:36.139
in infrastructure loans, which the Maldives
are finding increasingly hard to pay back

00:33:36.139 --> 00:33:41.000
and that potentially puts them in a debt-trap
diplomacy situation. The US has promised a

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.200
different approach.&nbsp;

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:47.789
Our favorite nature narrator Sir David Attenborough
gifted 7-year-old Prince George a prehistoric

00:33:47.789 --> 00:33:53.070
shark tooth that he collected while on family
vacation in Malta more than 50 years ago.

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:58.379
Malta did not appreciate the gesture. Officials
said the 23-million-year-old fossil, which

00:33:58.379 --> 00:34:02.889
likely belonged to a megalodon, should rightfully
find its place among their own country’s

00:34:02.889 --> 00:34:07.110
cultural heritage. The Maltese eventually
formally decided to let it slide this time,

00:34:07.110 --> 00:34:10.400
opting to avoid making the young prince cry.&nbsp;

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:15.790
A couple cases of beer may sound like a party
in some countries, but in Brunei, for a foreigner,

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:20.940
it meant getting questioned by police. An
Indonesian national was found with 57 beers

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:26.410
in his car at a road stop, which was a problem
for him, because alcohol is actually illegal

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:31.020
in the country. There is an exception for
non-Muslim foreigners who are allowed to possess

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:36.900
up to 12 cans or 2 liters of beer, but he
had four times the limit… and that’s a

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:37.900
party foul.&nbsp;

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:42.950
It appears that fish off the coast of Belize
have tamed a shrimp species in the same-ish

00:34:42.950 --> 00:34:48.690
way that humans domesticated dogs—at least
according to new scientific research. Apparently,

00:34:48.690 --> 00:34:53.570
the damselfish aggressively defends a patch
of algae against all predators because that’s

00:34:53.570 --> 00:34:58.520
where it harvests food, but it allows mysid
shrimp to hang around. In turn, the shrimp

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:02.900
fertilize the algae so it’s better food
for the damselfish. After time, the shrimp

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:07.280
rely on the fish for protection, and that’s
domestication.

00:35:07.280 --> 00:35:12.180
Exploratory drilling near the Bahamas and
150 miles or 240 kilometers from the Florida

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:17.450
coast is set to take place soon, despite opposition
from environmental groups and a ban from the

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:22.260
United States on offshore drilling. That’s
because the Bahamas Petroleum Company has

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:27.580
leases on potential oil fields in the area,
and they’re exercising them. Some are worried

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:31.900
about the environmental effects should something
go wrong, as has been the case time and time

00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:36.590
again, but the Bahamas sees dollar signs that
it needs in order to compensate for the financial

00:35:36.590 --> 00:35:40.820
hit it took when tourism ground to a halt
this year.&nbsp;

00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:45.070
Researchers studying islanders in a less tropical
part of the world found one interesting, and

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:51.000
potentially evolutionary, trait. It seems
that 2% of Iceland’s residents can’t smell

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:56.630
foul fish. In fact, when presented with the
malodorous sample, they pleasantly detected

00:35:56.630 --> 00:36:02.330
caramel, rose, or potatoes. These scientific
human specimens possess a mutation that turns

00:36:02.330 --> 00:36:07.970
off their TAAR5 gene—you know, the one responsible
for making a protein that recognizes trimethylamine,

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:11.750
aka a chemical found in rotten fish, and sometimes
human sweat and urine.&nbsp;

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:17.550
Vanuatu is a country that’s been hit hard
by Mother Nature, having experienced two volcanic

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:22.470
eruptions and two category-five cyclones in
the past five years. Disaster relief is something

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:28.030
officials there are having to learn quickly
and execute often. To do so, one system they’ve

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:33.050
recently implemented is distributing emergency
relief funds to residents via blockchain technology

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:37.560
and tap-and-pay cards. The cards are loaded
with “money” that residents can use immediately,

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:42.150
and it avoids some of the red tape that typically
consumes relief efforts.&nbsp;

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:46.480
Racial reckoning sparked movements worldwide,
and that groundswell did not leave the island

00:36:46.480 --> 00:36:51.570
of Barbados untouched. This most Eastern island
in the Caribbean formally announced it would

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:56.960
remove Queen Elizabeth as its head of state
and become a republic in 2021, citing long-simmering

00:36:56.960 --> 00:37:02.010
tensions about the legacy of colonization
and racial injustice. Some see the island’s

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:06.020
decision to part with the monarchy as a tipping
point for neighboring countries who may also

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:07.650
seek their own independence.

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:13.370
To help young women in Sao Tome and Principe,
the World Bank announced its commitment of

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:18.600
$15 million to empower young girls. Providing
safe learning spaces in schools is one of

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:22.560
the immediate goals of the funding, where
dropping out and pregnancy are common for

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:27.750
girls at a young age. Concurrently, but unrelated
to the funding, the country’s national Olympic

00:37:27.750 --> 00:37:32.660
committee announced a push to encourage girls
ages 6 to 18 into surfing lessons—another

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:37.340
way to steer young women into positive alternatives,
and potentially build their summer Olympics

00:37:37.340 --> 00:37:38.340
team.&nbsp;

00:37:38.340 --> 00:37:43.070
Samoa’s state-owned flag carrier Samoa Airways
was reporting financial trouble at a loss

00:37:43.070 --> 00:37:49.230
of $25 million in 2019, according to reports.
That was before this year, and the decimation

00:37:49.230 --> 00:37:53.990
of passenger demand—and so it’s not looking
any better for them. The airline also reportedly

00:37:53.990 --> 00:37:59.790
owes $11 million to creditors, but, of course,
executives are painting a rosier picture.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:05.560
This all comes as Samoa Airways tries to complete
its lease negotiations for a Boeing 737-800

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.910
from the Netherlands, and further debt could
hinder those.&nbsp;

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:13.590
When it comes to the world’s top honeymoon
destination, there’s one place that keeps

00:38:13.590 --> 00:38:20.470
blowing every other location out of international
waters, so to speak: St. Lucia. The Caribbean

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:25.100
island was named the number one honeymoon
locale for the twelfth time, and third consecutive

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:30.540
year. The World Travel Awards were announced
virtually in Moscow this year, and hopefully,

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:35.740
by this time next year, people won’t have
to take a virtual honeymoon to St. Lucia.&nbsp;

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:41.120
Climate change is impacting all islands, including
the 32 atolls that comprise the nation of

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:45.950
Kiribati. That’s why President Tanati Maamau
will accept funding from China to dredge sand

00:38:45.950 --> 00:38:50.810
along the most populous islands to actually
raise them out of the sea. Some see this as

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:55.430
a strategic play by China to inch further
into the Pacific Ocean, where military presence

00:38:55.430 --> 00:39:00.480
closer to the United States would definitely
be a power move. Maamau, however, says Kiribati

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:04.520
would not grant China the gift of building
a base on its nearby Christmas Island, which

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:08.470
just happens to be relatively close to Hawaii.&nbsp;

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:14.150
The Federated States of Micronesia are composed
of more than 600 islands stretching 1,600

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:19.390
miles or 2,700 kilometers across the Pacific
Ocean. The country’s population is quite

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:24.210
small in comparison to the vastness that gives
it the 14th largest exclusive economic zone

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:28.930
in the world. This is what makes the rescue
of three stranded men who wrote “SOS”

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:34.700
in the sand on a deserted atoll most impressive.
They’d run out of fuel and drifted 118 miles

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:38.790
or 190 kilometers west of their departure
point, and were spotted by a search plane

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:41.980
three days after their departure.&nbsp;

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:45.940
Another stranded-island story transports us
to Grenada, where a British woman visiting

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:50.770
the Caribbean island found herself stuck thanks
to the spring’s travel bans. The problem

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:55.750
is that she was six months pregnant, and ended
up delivering her child on the island, prematurely.

00:39:55.750 --> 00:40:00.030
She ended up securing a repatriation flight
in June; becoming one of tens of thousands

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:03.180
of British who were chartered back from at
least 57 countries.&nbsp;

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:10.170
We’re getting to the smaller countries and
most of them are islands. During 2015, a 30-mile,

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:15.070
50-kilometer algal bloom made a slow water-surface
crawl into the Pacific and scientists were

00:40:15.070 --> 00:40:20.500
baffled by what caused it, until a report
was released this year. The patch was created

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:26.740
by Tonga island runoff, rich in iron and phosphorus.
This is common, but its delayed appearance

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:32.240
is not, and that’s what confused scientists.
It took some time for a nitrogen fixer to

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:36.220
utilize the nutrients, making it seem like
the bloom came out of nowhere when it had

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:39.830
actually slow-cooked it’s way across the
ocean.&nbsp;

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:43.770
Travel between Ukraine and St Vincent and
the Grenadines became easier following the

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:48.290
signing of a visa-free travel agreement between
the two countries. The Caribbean country is

00:40:48.290 --> 00:40:52.770
part of the Commonwealth of Nations, a political
association of 54 members, nearly all former

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:57.000
territories of the British Empire. Ukraine
has its eyes on some of the more powerful

00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:01.090
Commonwealth members granting visa-free travel,
or at least the ambassador tweeted an allusion

00:41:01.090 --> 00:41:05.270
to that, with Australia, the UK, and Canada
on his wish list.&nbsp;

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:10.350
It’s been 44 years since the Seychelles
secured its independence from Britain, and

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:14.740
this year marked the first time that a president
from the opposition party won an election

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:20.120
since the country’s British breakaway. That’s
being heralded as a step toward greater democracy,

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:24.400
with Wavel Ramkalawan defeating an incumbent
from the United Seychelles party, which had

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.170
been in power since 1977.&nbsp;

00:41:27.170 --> 00:41:32.020
British sailor and 72-year-old man Graham
Walters was super-social distancing when he

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:36.430
sailed from Great Britain to Antigua, setting
the record for the oldest person to cross

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:41.130
the Atlantic Ocean solo. It was actually his
fifth trip across the ocean, and he narrowly

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:46.050
went awry at the end when strong winds blew
him six miles or 10 kilometers off course

00:41:46.050 --> 00:41:50.390
of Antigua. He was able to call the coast
guard and get a tow to his final destination

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:54.790
and officials still counted it as a world
record even with the assist.&nbsp;

00:41:54.790 --> 00:42:00.791
One of the tiniest European countries, Andorra,
joined the big-guns and became the 190th nation

00:42:00.791 --> 00:42:06.040
to become a part of the International Money
Fund. But why now? Well, crisis financing,

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:10.650
mostly, which the IMF comes through on for
countries in need, among other missions. Trade

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:15.290
and tourism—mostly ski resorts and hiking
trails—contribute to 40% of the mountainous

00:42:15.290 --> 00:42:18.920
country’s economy, and they’re hurting
right now with lack thereof.&nbsp;

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:23.280
A Caribbean island that’s counting on tourism
and the economy bouncing back is Dominica.

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:28.230
It announced its intentions to build a new
long-haul airport, capitalizing on a growing

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:33.020
eco-tourism and offshore financial sector.
Currently visitors must transfer to Dominica

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:36.720
through a neighboring island, but this new
airport would make direct trips from the United

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:41.820
States, United Kingdom, and Western Europe
more possible. Officials say it will be largely

00:42:41.820 --> 00:42:45.910
financed by foreigners who pay in exchange
for a passport, but it seems that we’ve

00:42:45.910 --> 00:42:47.960
heard that story before.

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:51.820
What drifts away from the coast of Mexico
likely ends up at the Marshall Islands, according

00:42:51.820 --> 00:42:57.580
to computer simulations. In this case, it
was a boat full of cocaine. Without any evidence

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:02.910
or leads, law enforcement officials have theorized
that a ghost boat filled with 1,430 pounds

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:07.630
or 650 kilograms of the illegal drug came
from the Mexican or Central American coast,

00:43:07.630 --> 00:43:12.170
and it had probably been floating captain-less
for at least a year. Though drugs have washed

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:17.620
up on shore before, this was the largest ever
haul and it was quickly incinerated.&nbsp;

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:21.670
It seems that Caribbean islands are all vying
for new citizens and the influx of money they

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:27.180
bring. St. Kitts and Nevis has made the process
so easy that wealthy investors can get citizenship

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:31.770
in under 60 days—one of the fastest tracks
in the world—without even having to step

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:35.790
foot in the country, and for that they’ve
been recognized by The Financial Times for

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:41.700
having one of the best citizen-by-investment
programs. For $150,000, a family of four can

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:46.070
call Saint Kitts their second, or third, or
fourth, home.&nbsp;

00:43:46.070 --> 00:43:50.651
Sir Jim Ratcliffe is another wealthy individual
moving to sunnier skies; he’s leaving the

00:43:50.651 --> 00:43:56.890
United Kingdom for Monaco, where he’ll save
up to $5.4 billion in taxes. Ratcliffe is

00:43:56.890 --> 00:44:03.130
a petrochemicals magnate with an estimated
worth of $23.6 billion. People who live in

00:44:03.130 --> 00:44:08.520
Monaco for at least 183 days a year don’t
pay income or property taxes, and that’s

00:44:08.520 --> 00:44:14.700
a lot different from the up to 45% he could
be paying under the UK’s tax system.&nbsp;

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:19.600
Sandwiched between Austria and Switzerland,
Liechtenstein said no to developing an international

00:44:19.600 --> 00:44:25.610
rail system. The country is only 15 miles,
or 25 kilometers, long and has just one domestic

00:44:25.610 --> 00:44:29.930
track. This initiative would have widened
it and upgraded one of its terminals so that

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:34.710
international trains could stop there, but
voters turned down the project at the polls--balking

00:44:34.710 --> 00:44:37.830
at the $73.5 million price tag.&nbsp;

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:42.500
With a 6 pm curfew in Italy due to lockdowns,
residents looking for a little nightlife are

00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:47.400
hopping the border into San Marino, where
bars and restaurants are open until 10 pm.

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:51.440
The small, independent country is just 10
minutes from cities such as Rimini, and Italian

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:55.500
officials are frustrated that by San Marino
not falling in line with their own guidelines,

00:44:55.500 --> 00:44:58.960
they’re putting more citizens at risk of
spreading the virus.&nbsp;

00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:04.960
Palau, about 1,000 miles or 1,600 kilometers
from the Philippines, has a free-association

00:45:04.960 --> 00:45:09.580
agreement with the United States that delivers
significant financial aid, and gives Palauans

00:45:09.580 --> 00:45:14.710
the right to travel to, live, and work in
the US. But that current compact expires in

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:21.650
2024, and Palau wants even more US presence—they’ve
invited America to build a military base on

00:45:21.650 --> 00:45:26.540
their island. Currently Palau has no military,
and they see an American base there as a way

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:30.960
to bolster security, and for the United States,
it’s a way to push back against growing

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:33.240
Chinese influence in the region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:38.260
The Perth Mint in Australia produces Tuvalu’s
currency, including the Tuvalu dollar, which

00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:43.140
is used alongside the Australian dollar, and
a series of collectible coins. This year,

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:47.120
Tuvalu issued a coin of American film star
John Wayne that has been selling for more

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:53.030
than $100. It turns out, the collectible coin
business is quite lucrative for the tiny island,

00:45:53.030 --> 00:45:59.230
which receives about $200,000 a year in royalties.
The coins are technically legal tender, but

00:45:59.230 --> 00:46:03.110
moreso they’re purchased by international
collectors.

00:46:03.110 --> 00:46:07.750
Australia uses the tiny Micronesian island
of Nauru as an outpost for refugees to spend

00:46:07.750 --> 00:46:12.190
time while their claims are processed. In
combination with Papua New Guinea, where detainees

00:46:12.190 --> 00:46:17.610
are also kept, there are about 300 people
held, but Australia spent $1.2 billion this

00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:22.570
financial year to keep them there, equally
about $4 million per person. This is causing

00:46:22.570 --> 00:46:26.180
many to turn their heads at the exorbitant
costs that would be better spent just keeping

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:31.240
those refugees in Australia. On top of that,
conditions in the refugee camps are poor and

00:46:31.240 --> 00:46:35.320
sometimes dangerous, so the money has not
been well spent.&nbsp;

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:40.910
And here we are, the world’s smallest country:
the Vatican City. This year, it has a very

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:46.510
2020 story: Catholicism’s headquarters may
have missed the mark on the Nativity scene.

00:46:46.510 --> 00:46:52.420
Each year the holy city produces a new scene
donated by an Italian city. This year Castelli,

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:56.800
a town known for ceramics, got the spotlight,
but the curious stylings of coiled clay meet

00:46:56.800 --> 00:47:01.810
astronauts have received curious reviews.
Castellians made the figures over decades,

00:47:01.810 --> 00:47:06.510
and they’ve survived natural disasters and
more. One observer saw hope in the scene:

00:47:06.510 --> 00:47:11.270
“It’s a Nativity scene that has had problems,
like we’ve all had a lousy year,” she

00:47:11.270 --> 00:47:14.340
said. “If it made it, we can.”

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:18.430
And that’s it--that’s the news you missed
in 2020, or at least some of it, from every

00:47:18.430 --> 00:47:22.840
country in the world. If you’ve made it
to this point, you’re clearly a lover of

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:26.970
learning about the world. Therefore, I’m
going to recommend you an audiobook from our

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:31.710
sponsor, Audible, that I’d honestly be stunned
if someone who made it through all 195 countries

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:36.290
has not already listened to. It’s Prisoners
of Geography, by Tim Marshall, which is this

00:47:36.290 --> 00:47:40.870
fascinating read about how geography constrains
countries--it explains things like why Putin

00:47:40.870 --> 00:47:45.450
is so focused on Crimea, why Europe will never
be united, and why the US was destined to

00:47:45.450 --> 00:47:49.930
become a superpower. You can actually listen
to this completely for free by signing up

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:55.390
for a free trial to Audible Premium Plus at
Audible.com/Wendover. That subscription includes

00:47:55.390 --> 00:47:59.620
the traditional credit system that allows
you to get any audiobook in their catalogue,

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:04.640
plus the new way to consume audio entertainment:
Audible Plus. This gives you unlimited access

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:09.160
to a massive catalogue of Audiobooks, Audible
Originals, podcasts, and more, which gives

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:14.610
you great content that can entertain and educate
you anytime your ears are free. If you’re

00:48:14.610 --> 00:48:19.020
just interested in Audible Plus, which is
a fantastic deal by itself, Audible has an

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:24.210
even better deal just for the holidays. By
